```css
:root {
  /* clamp(min, preferred, max) — fluid type in one line */
  --h1-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);

  /* min() picks the smaller: caps a width but allows shrink */
  --content-width: min(72ch, 100% - 2rem);

  /* max() picks the larger: guarantees a floor */
  --tap-target: max(44px, 3rem);
}

.sidebar {
  /* calc mixes units that plain CSS cannot reconcile */
  width: calc(100% - var(--nav-width, 240px) - 2 * 1.5rem);
}

.hero {
  margin-top: clamp(1rem, 4vh + 1rem, 4rem);
}
```

**Why this works:** `calc()` evaluates an expression mixing units (`%` minus `px`), which is impossible in a plain value — it resolves at computed-value time against the actual layout. `min()`/`max()` are just comparisons that resolve the same way; the crucial mental model is that they behave *opposite* to their names for sizing: `min(72ch, 100% - 2rem)` caps a maximum width (the element can still be smaller), while `max(44px, 3rem)` sets a minimum. `clamp(MIN, VAL, MAX)` is exactly `max(MIN, min(VAL, MAX))` and is the workhorse for fluid values: the example heading scales with the viewport between hard floors and ceilings, replacing a whole ladder of media-query font sizes. Inside `clamp` the middle term can itself be an expression (`4vh + 1rem`) — that offset keeps text from getting too small relative to line height at the extremes. All three functions accept `var()` references, which is how design-token systems stay responsive.

Specificity is a three-column score: (inline, id, class/attr/pseudo-class, element). When two rules target the same element, the higher score wins regardless of file order; equal scores fall back to source order. !important overrides everything and should be a last resort.

```css
/* id (1-0-0) beats class (0-1-0) */
#hero .title { color: red; }
.title { color: blue; } /* loses */
```

backdrop-filter applies a filter to everything painted behind the element, enabling frosted-glass effects over images or gradients. The element's own background must be semi-transparent for it to be visible.

```css
.modal {
  background: rgb(15 23 42 / 0.6);
  backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
}
```

```css
.alert {
  /* Physical (direction-blind): */
  margin-left: auto;
  padding-left: 1rem;
  border-left: 4px solid currentColor;
  text-align: left;

  /* Logical (adapts to writing mode): */
  margin-inline-start: auto;
  padding-inline-start: 1rem;
  border-inline-start: 4px solid currentColor;
  text-align: start;
}

/* Axis shorthand pair: */
.section {
  padding-block: 2rem;   /* top + bottom */
  margin-inline: auto;   /* left + right */
}
```

**Why this works:** Physical properties (`left`, `right`, `top`, `bottom`) are pinned to the screen's geometry, but in right-to-left languages like Persian and Arabic "left" and "right" swap meaning — a card built with `padding-left` looks correct in English and broken in RTL. Logical properties map to *flow-relative* axes: `inline` is the axis text flows along, `block` is the axis lines stack along, and `start`/`end` flip automatically with `direction` or `writing-mode`. The exact same stylesheet therefore mirrors itself for free under `dir="rtl"`, with no second RTL stylesheet and no `[dir="rtl"]` override blocks. `inset-inline-start` likewise replaces `left` for positioned elements. The `*-block`/`*-inline` shorthands also set both sides in one declaration. New projects should standardize on logical properties; the physical ones remain only for genuinely screen-anchored cases like `position: fixed` overlays relative to the viewport.

**Building a navbar with Flexbox — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
A navbar is a flex row: brand on one side, links and actions on the other.
```html
<nav class="nav"><a class="nav__brand" href="/">Site</a><ul class="nav__links"><li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li></ul></nav>
```
```css
.nav { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; padding: .75rem 1rem; }
.nav__links { display: flex; gap: 1rem; list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
```
Why it works: `space-between` pushes the brand and link group to opposite edges.

[Intermediate]
Make it responsive by letting links wrap and collapsing into a menu at small widths.
```css
@media (max-width: 48rem) {
  .nav__links { flex-direction: column; width: 100%; display: none; }
  .nav.is-open .nav__links { display: flex; }
}
```
Why it works: toggling one class switches the same flex container between row and column layouts.

[Advanced]
Handle overflow safely: `min-width: 0` on items, ellipsis on long labels, focus styles for keyboard users.
```css
.nav a { min-width: 0; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }
.nav a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--brand, #2563eb); outline-offset: 2px; }
```
Why it works: truncation prevents layout blowouts while `:focus-visible` keeps navigation usable without a mouse.

[Expert]
Ship it as an accessible landmark: skip-link target, `aria-expanded` on the toggle, and reduced-motion for the dropdown.
```html
<button class="nav__toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="menu">Menu</button>
```
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .nav__links { transition: none; } }
```
Why it works: assistive tech reads the toggle state from `aria-expanded`, so behavior and semantics stay in sync.

