**Why this works:**
- `place-content: center` on the hero grid vertically and horizontally centers the content without absolute positioning.
- `clamp()` makes the headline fluid: it grows with the viewport between the min and max bounds, so no extra media query is needed.
- The navbar uses `backdrop-filter: blur()` over a semi-transparent background (via `color-mix`) for a glass effect while staying readable.
- The mobile menu is progressive enhancement: it is hidden by CSS below 720px and toggled with `aria-expanded` kept in sync for screen readers.
- Cards use `auto-fit` + `minmax(240px, 1fr)` so the grid reflows from 1 to 3 columns automatically.

**What was wrong**
- Three selectors share an identical declaration block — copy-paste CSS drifts the first time someone edits one copy.
- Magic values (`#1e293b`, `22px`, `12px`) have no shared meaning or scale.

**Improved code**
```css
:root {
  --text-strong: #1e293b;
  --fs-heading: 1.375rem;
  --space-2: 1rem;
}
.heading,
.t1,
.t2,
.price {
  color: var(--text-strong);
  font-size: var(--fs-heading);
  margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
}
.price { font-weight: 700; }
```
```html
<h2 class="heading">Plan title</h2>
<p class="heading price">$9</p>
```

Why it's better: one declaration block (selector list) plus one composable modifier means a single source of truth, and tokens make the next theme change a two-line edit instead of a grep-and-pray hunt.

Instead of attaching a listener to every child, attach one to the parent and inspect event.target.closest(). Fewer listeners, less memory, and it keeps working for elements added later.

```css
list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  const item = e.target.closest("li");
  if (item) item.classList.toggle("done");
});
```

